# ploaness owns this file. Edit the harness, not this copy: `ploaness verify` compares it byte for byte
# and `ploaness sync` restores it.
#
# Docker sends the whole directory to the daemon before it reads the first instruction, so what is left
# out here is decided before any build step can decide it. Without this file a Payload project sends
# well over a gigabyte, nearly all of it dependencies and build output the image rebuilds anyway.
#
# The entry that matters most is `.env`. The stock Payload Dockerfile does `COPY . .` in its builder
# stage, so an env file present in the context is copied into that layer and kept in the build cache.
# The final image takes only `public` and `.next/standalone` and so does not carry it, but a single
# edit to the Dockerfile would change that. Secrets belong in the runtime environment, never in a layer.

# Dependencies and build output: the image installs and builds these itself.
node_modules
.next
.turbo
storybook-static

# Version control and its metadata.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Secrets. Never copied into a layer; supplied by the runtime instead.
.env
.env.*

# Test and coverage output, which no image needs.
coverage
test-results
playwright-report
.type-coverage

# The build recipe describes the image; it does not belong inside it.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.yaml
compose.yml
compose.yaml

# Editor and operating-system leftovers.
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
*.log
